ne through the entire array, with the largest element being moved to the end of the array.\n4. We then repeat this process for the remaining unsorted portion of the array (i.e., from the first element up to the last unsorted element).\n5. This process is repeated until the entire array is sorted in ascending order.\n6. Finally, we return the sorted array.\n\nThis is known as Bubble Sort algorithm.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nCould you provide some information on the time complexity of your algorithm? Also, can you explain the worst-case scenario for your function?<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nSure! The time complexity of the Bubble Sort algorithm used in this function is O(n^2), which means that the time required to complete the algorithm increases quadratically with the size of the input array. In the worst-case scenario, the input array is completely unsorted, and therefore the inner loop of the algorithm will compare and swap every element in the array n times, where n is the length of the array. This means that the function will perform n^2 comparisons, and thus the time complexity of the algorithm is O(n^2). The Bubble Sort algorithm is not the most efficient sorting algorithm, but it is relatively simple to implement and can handle small to medium-sized arrays with reasonable efficiency. For very large arrays, there are faster and more efficient sorting algorithms that can be used, like Merg","tokens":["<","|","im","_","start","|>","system","\n","You"," are"," a"," helpful"," assistant",".<","|","im","_","end","|>","\n","<","|","im","_","start","|>","user","\n","Please"," create"," a"," JavaScript"," function"," that"," can"," sort"," an"," array"," of"," strings"," in"," ascending"," alphabet","ical"," order","."," Your"," function"," should"," not"," utilize"," any"," built","-","in"," sorting"," functions"," or"," libraries","."," The"," function"," should"," take"," in"," one"," parameter",","," an"," array"," of"," strings","."," The"," sorted"," ar